<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: digits,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	This function is used at the end of the &quot;color decoder&quot; game. When the game is over (whether the user wins or loses), the 4-color secret code should be displayed at the top of the game board where initially there are dark grey boxes.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>secret_code</pf> ( ) double — array of doubles; each number in this array is a number between 1 and 6, where each digit represents the corresponding color in <pf>color_list</pf></p>
			<p><pf>color_list</pf> number — is a string of 6 letters representing 6 among the standard colors MATLAB recognizes:</p>
			<p><pf>'b'</pf> number — = blue, <pf>'k'</pf> = black, <pf>'r'</pf> = red, <pf>'g'</pf> = green, <pf>'y'</pf> = yellow, <pf>'c'</pf> = cyan, <pf>'m'</pf> = magenta, <pf>'w'</pf> = white</p>
			<p><pf>num_attempts</pf> (double) double — is a number that indicates how many attempts the user get (and therefore where the dark grey boxes are positioned)</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>there</pf> number — are no function outputs; the dark grey boxes on the game board should be replaced with the secret code</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		INPUT
		[3 1 2 2], 'ygrbcm', 5
		PLOT
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
